    text: "A hurricane begins as a cluster of thunderstorms over warm tropical ocean water, usually at least 80 degrees Fahrenheit. The warm water heats the air above it, and the warm, moist air rises. As it climbs, it cools, and its water vapor condenses into clouds and rain. Condensation releases heat, which warms the surrounding air and causes it to rise even faster. Air near the surface rushes in to replace the rising air, and the cycle feeds itself.\n\nBecause Earth rotates, the incoming winds do not flow straight toward the center; instead, they curve, and the storm begins to spin. In the Northern Hemisphere, hurricanes turn counterclockwise. When its winds reach 74 miles per hour, the storm is officially classified as a hurricane. A hurricane weakens once it moves over land or cooler water, because it loses the warm, moist air that serves as its fuel.",
