    text: "The immune system protects the body by recognizing germs and producing antibodies, proteins that attach to a specific invader and help destroy it. The first time the body meets a new germ, this response can take a week or more, and during that time a person may become seriously ill.\n\nA vaccine gives the immune system a chance to prepare without facing that danger. Depending on the type, a vaccine may contain a weakened or killed form of a germ, a harmless piece of it, or instructions that lead the body's own cells to make such a piece. The immune system reacts as though the real germ had arrived. It produces antibodies and also creates memory cells, which can survive for years. If the actual germ enters the body later, these memory cells recognize it and launch a strong defense quickly, often before any symptoms appear. Some vaccines require booster doses to keep this protection strong.",
